Subject: one web context and one web process for all tabs, no sandbox

Upstream gives every new unrelated tab its own ephemeral web context: a network process and a web
process per tab, and nothing kept between runs. On the PS3 (256 MB of XDR) each extra web process
costs tens of MB, so every tab now shares one persistent context (cookies, local storage and the
disk cache under the XDG data and cache directories; cookies in SQLite, third-party ones refused)
and is created related to an open tab's view. WebKitGTK's GTK 3 API leaves process swapping on
cross-site navigation off unless asked, and a related view takes its relative's process, so all
tabs live in one WebKitWebProcess. The cost: one tab's crash (or Ctrl-Escape) takes the others'
pages with it, and they reload.

The bubblewrap sandbox is not turned on: it needs user, PID and network namespaces, which the
PS3's kernel lacks, and WebKit has no fallback (the web process doesn't start). The tab's
context label is hidden, since there is one session.

